\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$; the standard number fields have their usual meanings. All additional conventions are stated locally.


\paragraph{Definitions and conventions.} A shared multipartite state is a positive trace-one operator $\rho$ on $\bigotimes_i\mathcal H_{A_i}\otimes\bigotimes_{j=1}^r\mathcal H_{B_j}$. Sender $A_i$ encodes a classical message by a local unitary and sends its subsystem to its assigned receiver. Local operations and classical communication (LOCC) permit the receivers to exchange classical messages, but not to perform a joint quantum operation. The dense-coding capacity is the supremum asymptotic classical communication rate, in bits per use, achievable with vanishing decoding error in the chosen receiver protocol. The unassisted benchmark is $\sum_i\log_2\dim\mathcal H_{A_i}$. Bound entanglement means entanglement from which the relevant parties cannot distil singlets by LOCC.

\paragraph{Source scope and qualifications.} The capacity depends on whether receiver decoding is local, LOCC, or global and on the assignment of senders to receivers. The report distinguishes these protocols; the question must retain that distinction. It asks both for more-than-two-receiver capacities or bounds and for usefulness of multipartite bound entanglement.

\paragraph{Catalogue mathematical target.}
\paragraph{Catalogue wording retained for traceability.}
For distributed dense coding with more than two receivers, determine the capacity, or at least a nontrivial upper bound. Can multipartite bound-entangled states be useful for distributed dense coding?

\paragraph{Saved literature context.} The supplied catalogue's assessment dated 2026-08-21 records: The single-receiver capacity is exact and the two-receiver case has nontrivial upper bounds, but the general more-than-two-receiver capacity and usefulness of multipartite bound entanglement remain unresolved in the literature checked. The assessment is retained as dated provenance; it is not a new comprehensive literature or open-status certification.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Determine how much information several separated receivers can decode, and whether undistillable multipartite entanglement can help.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath, record 30000176 (OWR-785-002). Catalogue curation under CC BY 4.0; definitions and English exposition added locally.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Entanglement and Decoherence: Mathematics and Physics of Quantum Information and Computation (2005), Oberwolfach Reports, DOI 10.4171/owr/2005/04, pp. 185--255; official report source and mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2005/04}.
\item[S3] Official EMS Press PDF of the same Oberwolfach report; PDF pages 19--21, printed pages 203--205.
\url{https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/45978}.
\item[S4] Bruss et al., Dense coding with multipartite quantum states; reference in the saved dated catalogue literature review.
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0507146}.
\item[S5] Das et al., distributed dense coding with two receivers; reference in the saved dated catalogue literature review.
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6247}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:30000176}

